--TEST--
The record `await` answers is a shape like any anonymous object a program writes: `Core\Debug::render` prints its sorted field names, `Core\Json::encode` writes a failed child's `error` record as a JSON object, and the encoder walks into the record's own fields — `rule:types/anonymous-object`, `rule:security/isolate-shares-nothing`
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.script]
spawn = true
--FILE adder.nvs--
<?nvs
return 3;
--FILE thrower.nvs--
<?nvs
throw new RuntimeError("the child failed");
--FILE--
<?nvs
use Core\Debug;
use Core\Json;

// A child that ends well. Its record has four fields, and `error` is `null`.
var $done = await spawn script "adder.nvs" with(output: "capture");
echo Debug::render($done), "\n";

// A child that throws. Its `error` field is a record with `class` and `message`.
var $failed = await spawn script "thrower.nvs" with(output: "capture");
echo Debug::render($failed->error), "\n";
echo Json::encode($failed->error), "\n";

// The record is an object to the encoder, so it reaches the `output` field.
// That field is terminal text, which has no JSON form.
try {
    echo Json::encode($done), "\n";
} catch (LogicError $text) {
    echo $text->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
$shape{error,ok,output,value}#1 (4) {
  $error => null
  $ok => bool(true)
  $output => Core\Cli\Text#2 (2) {
    $text => string(0) ""
    $runs => array(2) [
      0 => string(0) ""
      1 => null
    ]
  }
  $value => int(3)
}
$shape{class,message}#1 (2) {
  $class => string(12) "RuntimeError"
  $message => string(16) "the child failed"
}
{"class":"RuntimeError","message":"the child failed"}
Core\Json::encode(): an instance of `Core\Cli\Text` has no JSON encoding — a class participates by carrying `#[Json\Derive]` or by writing `toJson` itself
